{"p":"/audio/effects","t":"Audio effects","d":"Audio effects modify or enhance audio streams in your games.","h":"<p>Audio effects non-destructively modify or enhance audio streams. You can apply these effects to make your audio more immersive within your game, such as using an <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioEqualizer\"><code>AudioEqualizer</code></a> object to make rain sound muffled, <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioCompressor\"><code>AudioCompressor</code></a> object to control a sound's maximum volume, or <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioReverb\"><code>AudioReverb</code></a> to add more realistic reflections of sound in interior spaces.</p>\n<h2>Apply audio effects</h2>\n<p>You can apply audio effects to your audio streams by wiring up the effect within your 2D audio or 3D audio setup before it reaches the player's ears. For example, to review the setup instructions in <a href=\"#/docs/audio/objects\">Audio objects</a>, 2D audio requires an audio player to produce the stream, a physical hardware device like a speaker or headphones, and a wire to carry the audio stream from the audio player to the output device.</p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<p>If you keep this setup the way it is, the audio stream from the asset ID plays as it was originally recorded. However, if you want to apply an audio effect to this audio stream, you must introduce the audio effect in the middle of the configuration so that the audio stream transmits through the effect before it transmits to the output device.</p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<p>Roblox's modular audio objects also allow you to wire multiple audio players through an audio effect, meaning that you don't have to have multiple audio effect objects with the same settings. This provides a lot of flexibility in how you customize multiple audio sources at once.</p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<p>In addition, you can apply multiple audio effects to the same audio stream to further customize your audio. As the audio stream transmits from the audio player to the device output, it's important to note that the order of the audio effects impacts the custom sound. For example, if you were to apply an <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioChorus\"><code>AudioChorus</code></a> effect first, then an <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioDistortion\"><code>AudioDistortion</code></a> effect second, your custom sound may sound different from a configuration that applies the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioDistortion\"><code>AudioDistortion</code></a> effect before the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioChorus\"><code>AudioChorus</code></a> effect.</p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<p>Each audio effect you apply has additional properties you can adjust until you achieve the type of sound you desire. For more information on these properties, see each audio effect's API page.</p>\n<h2>Types</h2>\n<h3>Equalizer</h3>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioEqualizer\"><code>AudioEqualizer</code></a> allows for control of the volume of various frequency ranges. This audio effect is useful to highlight or minimize particular elements of audio, such as muffling all audio in your game when a player goes underwater.</p>\n<div class=\"gridc\">\n<figure>\n    <figcaption>Without <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioEqualizer\"><code>AudioEqualizer</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n<figure>\n<figcaption>With <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioEqualizer\"><code>AudioEqualizer</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<h3>Compressor</h3>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioCompressor\"><code>AudioCompressor</code></a> reduces the dynamic range of audio by lowering the volume of the highest parts of a source while at the same time raising the overall volume. This audio effect is useful to set a consistent volume for players to hear even if you record audio while whispering or speaking loudly. You can also use this dynamic effect to prioritize audio streams over another through the process of ducking.</p>\n<div class=\"gridc\">\n<figure>\n    <figcaption>Without <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioCompressor\"><code>AudioCompressor</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n<figure>\n<figcaption>With <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioCompressor\"><code>AudioCompressor</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<h3>Reverb</h3>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioReverb\"><code>AudioReverb</code></a> simulates the effect of sounds bouncing off of several surfaces. This audio effect is useful to simulate more realistic reflections of sounds in interior spaces, such as bouncing a basketball inside a gymnasium, or playing rock music in a stadium.</p>\n<div class=\"gridc\">\n<figure>\n    <figcaption>Without <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioReverb\"><code>AudioReverb</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n<figure>\n<figcaption>With <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioReverb\"><code>AudioReverb</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<h3>Chorus</h3>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioChorus\"><code>AudioChorus</code></a> simulates the effect of multiple vocals or instruments playing simultaneously by taking the original sound and overlaying copies, each with slight variations in pitch. This audio effect is useful for simulating a robotic or futuristic quality to your audio.</p>\n<div class=\"gridc\">\n<figure>\n    <figcaption>Without <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioChorus\"><code>AudioChorus</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n<figure>\n<figcaption>With <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioChorus\"><code>AudioChorus</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<h3>Distortion</h3>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioDistortion\"><code>AudioDistortion</code></a> simulates the effect that would occur when overdriving older style audio equipment. This audio effect causes clipping in the sound and adds a general \"fuzziness,\" which is useful for either adding intensity and character to musical instruments.</p>\n<div class=\"gridc\">\n<figure>\n    <figcaption>Without <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioDistortion\"><code>AudioDistortion</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n<figure>\n<figcaption>With <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioDistortion\"><code>AudioDistortion</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<h3>Echo</h3>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioEcho\"><code>AudioEcho</code></a> causes a sound to repeat on a delay with diminishing volume, simulating the real effect of an echo that bounces off of hard surfaces, such as walls and caves.</p>\n<div class=\"gridc\">\n<figure>\n    <figcaption>Without <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioEcho\"><code>AudioEcho</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n<figure>\n<figcaption>With <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioEcho\"><code>AudioEcho</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<h3>Flanger</h3>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioFlanger\"><code>AudioFlanger</code></a> creates a sweeping or whooshing effect by copying the original audio signal and playing it slightly offset and modulated on top of the original. This audio effect is useful for adding strange, otherworldly digital quality to your audio. For example, you can use it on an air conditioner recording to make a sci‑fi spaceship engine rumble.</p>\n<div class=\"gridc\">\n<figure>\n    <figcaption>Without <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioFlanger\"><code>AudioFlanger</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n<figure>\n<figcaption>With <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioFlanger\"><code>AudioFlanger</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<h3>Pitch Shift</h3>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioPitchShifter\"><code>AudioPitchShifter</code></a> raises or lowers pitch without changing the playback speed. This audio effect is useful for changing the scale of your sounds. You can use it to make small sounds feel huge by pitching them down or conversely pitch sounds up to make an explosion sound more like a balloon pop.</p>\n<div class=\"gridc\">\n<figure>\n    <figcaption>Without <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioPitchShifter\"><code>AudioPitchShifter</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n<figure>\n<figcaption>With <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioPitchShifter\"><code>AudioPitchShifter</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<h3>Tremolo</h3>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioTremolo\"><code>AudioTremolo</code></a> creates a trembling effect on a sound by varying its volume up and down. This dynamic effect is useful to transform instrument sounds into a wavy, dreamlike quality, or to create swells and dips in weather‑related audio.</p>\n<div class=\"gridc\">\n<figure>\n    <figcaption>Without <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioTremolo\"><code>AudioTremolo</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n<figure>\n<figcaption>With <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioTremolo\"><code>AudioTremolo</code></a> effect</figcaption>\n    <br />\n    \n    </source>\n    </audio>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<h3>Fader</h3>\n<p>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioFader\"><code>AudioFader</code></a> adjusts the volume of audio streams to be either higher or lower. This audio effect is useful to control the volume and audio effects properties of multiple audio streams at once.</p>\n<h3>Analyzer</h3>\n<p>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AudioAnalyzer\"><code>AudioAnalyzer</code></a> analyzes volume and frequency contents. This audio effect is useful for getting information to understanding how your audio is working together, both in volume and frequency content.</p>","kid":{"simple":"Audio effects change how a sound comes out without changing the original file. You place an effect between the `AudioPlayer` and the speaker, so the sound passes through it on the way. This lets you make rain sound muffled, make a gym echo, or make a voice sound like a robot. You can chain several effects, and the order you put them in changes the result.","analogy":"Effects are like the filters on a photo app. The original photo stays the same, but each filter changes how it looks, and putting a black-and-white filter before or after a blur gives a slightly different picture.","keyPoints":["Effects go in the middle of the wiring: `AudioPlayer` to effect to output, each step joined by a `Wire`.","Several audio players can be wired into one effect, so you do not need a copy of the effect for each sound.","You can stack many effects on one sound, and their order matters.","`AudioEqualizer` raises or lowers low, middle, and high sounds, for example to make things sound muffled underwater.","`AudioCompressor` evens out loud and quiet parts so volume stays steady; `AudioReverb` adds room bounce, like in a gym.","`AudioEcho` repeats the sound with fading volume; `AudioChorus` and `AudioFlanger` layer slightly changed copies for robotic or sci-fi sounds.","`AudioDistortion` adds fuzz, `AudioPitchShifter` makes sounds higher or lower without changing speed, and `AudioTremolo` makes volume wobble.","`AudioFader` turns the volume of everything passing through up or down, and `AudioAnalyzer` measures volume and frequencies."],"terms":{"non-destructively":"The effect changes what players hear, but the sound file itself is never edited. Remove the effect and it sounds like the original again.","audio stream":"The sound traveling through wires from one audio object to the next.","frequency":"How high or low a sound is. A bass drum is low frequency; a whistle is high frequency.","dynamic range":"The gap between the quietest and loudest parts of a sound. A compressor makes that gap smaller.","ducking":"Automatically making one sound quieter so another one is easier to hear, like lowering music when someone talks.","reverb":"The many quick bounces of sound off walls that make a big room sound big.","clipping":"When a sound is pushed louder than the equipment can handle, so the peaks get chopped off and it sounds crunchy.","pitch":"How high or low a note sounds. Pitching down makes a small sound feel huge; pitching up can turn an explosion into a balloon pop.","modulated":"Changed back and forth over time, like a wobble.","gain":"How much louder or quieter a part of the sound is made, measured in decibels. On `AudioEqualizer` each band goes from -80 to 10."},"example":{"title":"Muffle music with an equalizer","place":"Script in ServerScriptService","code":"local SoundService = game:GetService(\"SoundService\")\n\nlocal player = Instance.new(\"AudioPlayer\")\nplayer.Asset = \"rbxassetid://1234567890\" -- replace with your audio asset ID\nplayer.Looping = true\nplayer.Parent = SoundService\n\n-- The effect sits in the middle\nlocal equalizer = Instance.new(\"AudioEqualizer\")\nequalizer.HighGain = -30 -- cut the high sounds a lot\nequalizer.MidGain = -10 -- cut the middle a little\nequalizer.Parent = SoundService\n\nlocal output = Instance.new(\"AudioDeviceOutput\")\noutput.Parent = SoundService\n\n-- Wire 1: player -> equalizer\nlocal wire1 = Instance.new(\"Wire\")\nwire1.SourceInstance = player\nwire1.TargetInstance = equalizer\nwire1.Parent = SoundService\n\n-- Wire 2: equalizer -> speakers\nlocal wire2 = Instance.new(\"Wire\")\nwire2.SourceInstance = equalizer\nwire2.TargetInstance = output\nwire2.Parent = SoundService\n\nwhile not player.IsReady do\n\ttask.wait(0.1)\nend\nplayer:Play()","explain":["We make an `AudioPlayer` with a sound to play.","We make an `AudioEqualizer` and lower the high and middle sounds, which makes music sound like it is behind a wall or underwater.","We make an `AudioDeviceOutput` so everyone can hear it.","The first wire goes from the player into the equalizer, and the second goes from the equalizer to the output.","Once the sound is loaded, we play it. Everyone hears the muffled version, but the original file is unchanged."]},"watchOut":["If you wire the player straight to the output, the effect is skipped. The sound must pass through the effect.","Swapping the order of two effects (for example chorus then distortion versus distortion then chorus) can change the sound, so try both.","Too many strong effects at once can make sound messy. Change one setting at a time and listen."]}}